
---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "@/components/nav.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/footer.astro";
---

<Layout title="Metasploitable Vulnerability Lab | Alfayo Robin">
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 pt-28 pb-12 text-[var(--white)]">
    <a
      href="/#projects"
      class="mb-8 inline-block text-sm text-[var(--sec)] transition hover:opacity-80"
    >
      ← Back to Projects
    </a>

    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)]">
        Cybersecurity • Vulnerability Assessment
      </p>

      <h1 class="mb-5 text-3xl font-bold md:text-5xl">
        Metasploitable Vulnerability Lab
      </h1>

<p class="max-w-3xl text-base leading-7 opacity-80 md:text-lg">
  A hands-on cybersecurity lab using Metasploitable 3 as a deliberately
  vulnerable target in a virtualised environment. The project focuses on
  understanding lab networking, checking connectivity between virtual
  machines, and learning how service discovery supports vulnerability
  assessment in a controlled testing environment.
</p>

      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Metasploitable</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">VirtualBox</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Kali Linux</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Vulnerability Assessment</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">1. Project Overview</h2>
      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        The objective of this lab was to become familiar with vulnerable
        systems and the process security analysts use to identify potential
        weaknesses. Metasploitable provides a deliberately vulnerable target
        that can be used for safe, controlled security testing in an isolated
        environment.
      </p>
    </section>

<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">2. Lab Environment</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
    <li><strong>Virtualization:</strong> Oracle VirtualBox.</li>
    <li><strong>Testing system:</strong> Kali Linux, used for security testing and network reconnaissance.</li>
    <li><strong>Target:</strong> Metasploitable 3, a deliberately vulnerable virtual machine.</li>
    <li><strong>Network configuration:</strong> Virtual machine network adapters configured for lab connectivity.</li>
    <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Practise network reconnaissance, understand exposed services, and learn vulnerability assessment in a controlled environment.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm opacity-70">
    The lab is intended for authorised testing within the isolated
    virtual environment. Machine IP addresses and network settings
    can be documented once verified.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">3. Setup and Methodology</h2>
  <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
    <li>
      <strong>Prepared the virtual environment.</strong>
      Oracle VirtualBox and Kali Linux were already installed, so I
      continued by setting up the Metasploitable 3 target virtual machine.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Configured the target machine.</strong>
      I configured the virtual machine's network settings to support
      communication within the lab environment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Verified network connectivity.</strong>
      I used connectivity checks to confirm communication between lab
      systems before proceeding with further testing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Planned service discovery.</strong>
      The next stage is to use tools such as Nmap to identify accessible
      ports and services on the authorised target. Specific scan commands
      and findings should be included only when verified.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Considered security implications.</strong>
      The assessment provides an opportunity to understand how exposed
      services, outdated software, and insecure configurations can
      increase the attack surface of a system.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    4. Screenshots and Evidence
  </h2>

  <p class="mb-6 max-w-3xl leading-7 opacity-80">
    The screenshots below document the Metasploitable lab environment,
    network configuration, Nmap scanning activity, and integration
    with Wazuh for security monitoring.
  </p>

  <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
    <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10">
      <img
        src="/images/Projects/Metasploitable/mn.png"
        alt="Metasploitable lab network configuration"
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full object-contain"
      />
      <figcaption class="p-4">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">1. Network Configuration</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 opacity-80">
          Shows the network configuration used for the virtual lab.
          Correct network settings are important for communication
          between virtual machines during controlled testing.
        </p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10">
      <img
        src="/images/Projects/Metasploitable/metasploitrun.png"
        alt="Terminal running during the Metasploitable lab exercise"
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full object-contain"
      />
      <figcaption class="p-4">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">2. Lab Terminal</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 opacity-80">
          Captures terminal activity during the lab exercise and
          provides evidence of hands-on command-line work.
        </p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10">
      <img
        src="/images/Projects/Metasploitable/Metasploit%20scan.png"
        alt="Nmap scan output from the Metasploitable lab"
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full object-contain"
      />
      <figcaption class="p-4">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">3. Nmap Scan</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 opacity-80">
          Shows Nmap scanning activity used to examine the target.
          Scan results can help identify accessible ports and
          services for further investigation.
        </p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10">
      <img
        src="/images/Projects/Metasploitable/Metonwazuh.png"
        alt="Metasploitable agent displayed as active in the Wazuh dashboard"
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full object-contain"
      />
      <figcaption class="p-4">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">4. Wazuh Agent Status</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 opacity-80">
          Shows the Metasploitable agent as active in Wazuh,
          demonstrating connectivity to the monitoring platform.
          Agent status alone does not establish that a specific
          security event was detected.
        </p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    5. Challenges and Considerations
  </h2>
  <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
    One important consideration during lab setup was ensuring that the
    virtual machines were configured correctly for network communication.
    Verifying connectivity before proceeding with security testing helped
    establish a reliable starting point for the assessment. Another key
    consideration was keeping the deliberately vulnerable target within
    a controlled lab environment to reduce the risk of exposing it to
    untrusted systems. This project reinforced the importance of checking
    network settings, confirming target availability, and documenting
    test results before drawing security conclusions.
  </p>
</section>


<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    6. Skills and Lessons Learned
  </h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
    <li>
      <strong>Virtual Lab Configuration:</strong>
      Understanding how virtual machines and network adapters support
      a controlled cybersecurity testing environment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Network Connectivity:</strong>
      Practising connectivity verification before proceeding with
      security testing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Service Discovery Concepts:</strong>
      Understanding how tools such as Nmap can identify accessible
      ports and services for further investigation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vulnerability Assessment:</strong>
      Learning how exposed services and insecure configurations can
      introduce security risks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Security Analysis and Documentation:</strong>
      Recognising the importance of recording evidence, validating
      observations, and relating findings to potential remediation.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-6">
 <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-semibold">7. Conclusion</h2>
  <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
    Setting up the Metasploitable 3 lab has helped me develop a better
    understanding of virtual lab configuration, network connectivity,
    and the role of service discovery in security testing. The project
    reinforces the importance of working within a controlled environment,
    verifying technical observations, and documenting evidence before
    drawing conclusions. I plan to build on this foundation through
    further hands-on testing and analysis as I continue developing my
    cybersecurity and security operations skills.
  </p>
</section>

  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>